const RULES = `
RULES THAT APPLY TO EVERYTHING YOU DO AND RETURN
1. OUTPUT FILE AND RESUME. Your result goes to ${OUT}/<your label>.json (create the folder if needed). If that file already exists and parses as a complete result for this task, return it and stop. Otherwise write it when you finish AND return it. Scripts you write go to ${OUT}/scripts/<your label>/ and must run as saved.
2. READ-ONLY ON THE REPOS. Never write, edit or commit anything inside ${REPO} or ${LEAN}. Scratch goes under ${OUT}/scratch/<your label>/. Downloaded data goes under ${DATA}/<short-name>/ on the D: drive, never on C:, at most 150 MB per dataset and 300 MB in total. Record the URL, the date, the byte size and the sha256 of every file you keep.
3. HEADROOM. This machine is shared and nearly full. Before any heavy local command (a Lean run, a download over 20 MB, anything using many cores) check free RAM and free disk; if free RAM is under 15 percent, free C: under 1 GB, or free D: under 3 GB, do NOT run it, say so in could_not_find, and carry on with what does not need it. Never run more than one Lean process at a time.
4. CITATIONS. Every citation carries a DOI or arXiv id that YOU fetched and saw resolve in this run (Crossref https://api.crossref.org/works/<doi>, DataCite https://api.datacite.org/dois/<doi>, arXiv export API, Europe PMC, PubMed, or the publisher page). Set verified=true only then, and say how in how_verified. Never supply a DOI from memory as verified. A shorter list of real citations beats a longer list with one invented entry. DO NOT use OpenAlex (its free budget for this network is exhausted). Do NOT put any email address, name or account into a request to an outside service; use a generic User-Agent such as "delphi-research/0.1". Do not sign up for anything, accept terms, or log in.
5. NUMBERS. Every number states where it came from: a page you fetched (URL), a file you read (path), or your own calculation (show the arithmetic, and save the script). A number from memory is labelled "unverified background" and is never used as a load-bearing claim. Where a source shows a figure rather than a number, say you could not read the value rather than estimating it.
6. EVIDENCE. For each finding and each key_claim give evidence_quote: the sentence or table row, copied verbatim from the fetched source, at most 40 words. If you could not copy the exact text, leave it empty and lower the confidence. A reviewer will independently re-fetch your key_claims and compare.
7. KEY CLAIMS. key_claims are the 2 to 4 statements everything else in your report depends on. Choose them honestly; a skeptic will test exactly these.
8. FROZEN HOLDOUT. This project has a frozen holdout: do NOT read, use, cite or mention [held-out clade], [held-out clade] species, feathers, beaks, or [held-out clade] flight or anatomy anywhere in your output, even where it is the natural comparison, and do not open any folder or file whose name refers to them (for example the morpholib dataset folders named for them). Use fish, mammals, insects, plants, seeds and engineered bodies instead.
9. REPRODUCIBLE. reproduce lists the exact commands or URLs someone else would run to get your numbers again, in order, and what they should see. If a step needs a tool, name it and its version. If it cannot be reproduced, say why.
10. LAYPERSON. lay_summary is 3 to 6 sentences a thoughtful non-scientist could follow: short sentences, no unexplained term, one concrete picture or comparison. glossary gives every technical word you used in the summary a one-line plain meaning.
11. HONESTY. Say plainly what you could not find or verify. An honest gap is a result. Do not smooth over a disagreement between sources; report both numbers and who said them.
`

